  "static_route_policies": ["unselected", "static", "dynamic"],
  "static_route_refusals": ["unverifiable", "unnamed_model", "unlisted_model", "ineligible_model", "effort_not_configurable", "unsupported_effort", "unsupported_context_tier"],
  "static_route_notes": {
    "policy": "Contract 2.8. The file-level contract_version above stays at the revision that first described the measured tier; these Static route cases arrived at 2.8 and say so here rather than moving a declaration that belongs to a different decision. The Route policy is SELECTED, never inferred. `unselected` is the absence of a decision and retains legacy routing interpretation -- the hardcoded roster gates, the built-in escalation rung, the historical event stream -- subject to the staged Python-local startup refusal described by migration_activation. Only `static` activates the cases below; `dynamic` selects the Dynamic route (contract 14.4), whose election is scored from live evidence rather than pinned here, and whose own record is pinned by event-schema.json's `wrapper.routing.resolved`. A Runner MUST NOT reinterpret an existing Config as though the policy had always been in force, and MUST NOT read the absence of a policy as a choice of one.",
    "migration_activation": "Python local Runs with nonempty project or global Config MUST supply or inherit an explicit static/dynamic policy before Agent work. Missing authority refuses without prompting or Config writes, naming update --routing keep/migrate and temporary --route-policy/GIT_LOOPY_ROUTE_POLICY remedies. Model/effort overrides alone are not consent. CLI startup, detached startup and Run/doctor preflight share this authority verdict; selected policies still require live readiness and fresh Pickup validation. Historical records, empty Config scopes and unselected non-local Runs retain their legacy path during staged activation. Shell and PowerShell enforcement is deferred and unchanged members remain conforming. This is not final Dynamic-default activation or remote, Subagent or Integration routing support. The one-route cases below do not model startup; migration_recovery drives the composed CLI/serial/Lane recovery seam.",
    "verifiable_placement": "The harness whose answer counts is the one the WORK SESSION runs under. Where an Execution host opens its sessions on a machine that authenticates as itself, the orchestrator's own listing describes a different installation under a different identity, and a Runner MUST refuse that combination before work rather than report a local read as that placement's verdict. Only a placement whose sessions run under the Run's own authenticated harness is verifiable.",
  "authority": "Under `static` the authority is the AUTHENTICATED HARNESS THIS RUN SPAWNS -- its own model listing, read fresh. A Runner MUST NOT validate a Static route against a hardcoded roster, a published plan or licence catalogue, or another CLI installation, and MUST NOT satisfy the read from a cache held across the Run: a stale answer is the failure this rule exists to prevent, not a cheaper version of it. Reading eligibility MUST NOT rewrite billing provenance already recorded for the Run.",
    "refuse_never_rescue": "Every verdict other than `accepted` ends the Run BEFORE any work, naming the selected settings. A Runner MUST NOT drop an unsupported effort, downgrade an unoffered context tier, or substitute a backend default and call the result a route: a Static route is honoured exactly or not at all.",
    "effort_dial_versus_effort_value": "`efforts: null` is the harness's own statement that the model has NO EFFORT DIAL, and a route on such a model MUST send no effort argument at all. That is a different fact from a model whose dial offers the value `none`, which MUST be sent as a value. A Runner that collapses the two either sends `none` to a model that cannot read it, or omits an effort the operator explicitly selected.",
    "unverifiable_is_not_empty": "`harness: null` is a capability read that did not complete. It is `unverifiable` -- NOT an empty harness, NOT a reason to fall back to a roster, and NOT a reason to proceed. A Runner MUST NOT treat an unanswered harness as agreement.",
    "static_is_fixed": "A Static route stays fixed for an Agent and across every permitted retry. A Runner MUST NOT promote it to a different model or effort unless the operator explicitly configured an escalation rung: a rung a Runner ships by default is not consent. Attempt and Strike accounting is unchanged either way.",
    "dynamic_precedence": "Under `dynamic` a Static route still wins. A [routing] entry, an explicit flag or environment pin, and a configured escalation rung are instructions, and a Runner MUST NOT spend a Route selector call to contradict one -- they are verified against the harness exactly as under `static`. What the selector elects is the route for every Task type those instructions do not cover; the run-wide default is NOT one of them, because under this policy the selector replaces it and refusing the Run over a pair it will never take would be a refusal about nothing. An explicit pin suppresses routing entirely, which makes the default the route again and therefore verifiable again.",
    "scope": "These cases validate ONE route. A Run validates every route its Config holds -- the run-wide default, each [routing] entry, and an explicitly configured escalation rung -- before it picks up any issue, so a route that is never exercised is still refused at the start rather than mid-Run."
  },
  "migration_recovery": {
    "note": "Every case starts with an unattended local Python Run refusing the saved Config unchanged, before listing, assessment or work. Supply the declared authority, then drive a real Run and compare actual issue-owning session settings, canonical Pickup and Dashboard readback with expected, or assert the declared refusal with no work, final route, publication or Strike. Static policy recovery has no leaderboard credential or evidence request. Temporary authority must expire on the next invocation; recorded authority must remain effective without flags or environment. No historical case or Event stream is reinterpreted. Shell/PowerShell activation is deferred; this is not remote, Subagent or Integration routing support or final default activation.",
    "inputs": "Case saved_config entries overlay the shared saved_config at top level. A case harness replaces the shared listing; each model's long_context defaults to false and must feed every Static and Dynamic capability read. run_overrides are temporary CLI settings, not saved authority. run_access and run_evidence_available default to true and change only after the authorization step; false respectively removes the operator-owned key or makes the evidence transport fail. proposed_context_tier defaults to default and scripts the selector's work proposal, never the selector's own settings. A missing expected.exit_code means 0 and one work session; a refusal case declares its nonzero exit, named Static/Dynamic refusal and Run outcome (null for refusal before a Run event stream exists). The adapter checks its CLI's diagnostic for that refusal. run_evidence_requests counts only the Run after authorization. The Dashboard retains its historical implicit-default-tier projection.",
    "modes": ["serial", "lane"],
